  var ECO21_QUESTION_BANK = [
    { q: 'Economic growth is best defined as:', options: ['An increase in the total output (real GDP) of an economy over time', 'A fall in the general price level', 'A rise in unemployment', 'A decrease in government spending'], correct: 0, explain: 'Economic growth refers to an increase in the value of goods and services produced by an economy, usually measured using real GDP.', misconception: 'Economic growth is about output increasing, not about prices or unemployment, which are separate macroeconomic variables.', tag: 'Economic growth' },
    { q: 'A key limitation of using GDP to measure a country’s economic wellbeing is that it:', options: ['Does not directly show how income is distributed between rich and poor', 'Always overstates the true level of poverty', 'Cannot be measured in any country', 'Only applies to developing economies'], correct: 0, explain: 'GDP measures total output but says nothing about how that output/income is shared, so a rise in GDP might not benefit everyone equally.', misconception: 'GDP is a real, widely used measure — its limitation is what it fails to capture (distribution, wellbeing), not that it cannot be measured.', tag: 'Economic growth' },
    { q: 'During a "boom" phase of the economic cycle, an economy typically experiences:', options: ['High output, low unemployment and rising inflationary pressure', 'Falling output and rising unemployment', 'The lowest point of economic activity', 'Zero economic activity'], correct: 0, explain: 'A boom is the peak of the economic cycle, characterised by high output and demand, low unemployment, and often rising inflation.', misconception: 'Falling output and rising unemployment describe a downturn or recession, not a boom, which is the high point of the cycle.', tag: 'Economic growth' },
    { q: 'A recession is generally defined as:', options: ['A sustained fall in real GDP, often over two consecutive quarters', 'Any single day of falling share prices', 'A rise in economic growth', 'A period of zero inflation'], correct: 0, explain: 'A recession is typically defined as a significant, sustained decline in economic output, commonly measured as at least two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP.', misconception: 'A recession is a sustained economic downturn, not a single short-term event like one day’s stock market movement.', tag: 'Economic growth' },
    { q: 'A likely impact of positive economic growth is:', options: ['Rising average living standards, as more goods and services become available', 'Guaranteed elimination of all poverty', 'A permanent fall in the price level', 'Automatic environmental improvement'], correct: 0, explain: 'Economic growth generally raises incomes and output, which tends to improve average living standards, though it does not automatically solve poverty or protect the environment.', misconception: 'Growth is linked to rising living standards on average, but it does not guarantee that poverty disappears or the environment improves.', tag: 'Economic growth' },
    { q: 'Inflation is best defined as:', options: ['A sustained rise in the general (average) price level over time', 'A one-off increase in the price of a single good', 'A fall in the general price level', 'An increase in unemployment'], correct: 0, explain: 'Inflation refers to a sustained increase in the average price level across the economy, not just a change in one product’s price.', misconception: 'Inflation is about the general price level over time, not a single price change, and it is the opposite of deflation.', tag: 'Inflation' },
    { q: 'The Consumer Price Index (CPI) is used to measure inflation by:', options: ['Tracking the average change in price of a representative basket of goods and services over time', 'Measuring only the price of imported goods', 'Recording the exchange rate each day', 'Counting the number of businesses in an economy'], correct: 0, explain: 'The CPI tracks how the average price of a fixed "basket" of commonly bought goods and services changes over time, giving a measure of inflation.', misconception: 'CPI measures a broad basket of everyday goods and services, not just imports, exchange rates, or business numbers.', tag: 'Inflation' },
    { q: 'Demand-pull inflation occurs when:', options: ['Aggregate demand in the economy rises faster than the economy’s ability to supply goods and services', 'The cost of production rises, pushing up prices', 'Demand for a good falls sharply', 'Unemployment rises significantly'], correct: 0, explain: 'Demand-pull inflation happens when total demand in the economy outpaces supply capacity, pulling prices upward.', misconception: 'A rise in production costs causing prices to rise describes cost-push inflation, a different cause of inflation.', tag: 'Inflation' },
    { q: 'Cost-push inflation occurs when:', options: ['Rising costs of production, such as wages or raw materials, cause firms to raise prices', 'Consumer demand for goods and services rises sharply', 'The government reduces its own spending', 'Interest rates fall to very low levels'], correct: 0, explain: 'Cost-push inflation is caused by rising costs of production (for example wages or raw material prices), which firms pass on as higher prices.', misconception: 'A rise in demand causing inflation is demand-pull inflation, not cost-push, which is driven by the supply/cost side of the economy.', tag: 'Inflation' },
    { q: '"Shoe-leather costs" of inflation refer to:', options: ['The extra time and effort people spend trying to minimise the effects of falling money values, such as shopping around more', 'The cost of printing new banknotes', 'A tax on footwear', 'The cost of importing shoes'], correct: 0, explain: 'Shoe-leather costs describe the extra effort and time consumers and businesses spend adjusting their behaviour (for example, more frequent shopping around) to cope with inflation.', misconception: 'This is a named economic concept about the practical costs of coping with inflation, not a literal cost related to footwear.', tag: 'Inflation' },
    { q: '"Menu costs" of inflation refer to:', options: ['The costs businesses incur from having to frequently update prices, such as reprinting price lists', 'The cost of food eaten by workers', 'A government subsidy for restaurants', 'The cost of imported menus'], correct: 0, explain: 'Menu costs are the costs businesses face from constantly having to change prices (like literally reprinting menus or catalogues) during periods of inflation.', misconception: 'Menu costs are a named economic concept about repricing costs, unrelated to the literal cost of food.', tag: 'Inflation' },
    { q: 'Unemployment is best defined as:', options: ['People who are willing and able to work but cannot find a job', 'Anyone who is not currently in education', 'People who choose not to look for work at all', 'The total size of a country’s labour force'], correct: 0, explain: 'Unemployment refers to people who are able and willing to work, actively seeking employment, but unable to find a job.', misconception: 'Someone choosing not to seek work at all (economically inactive) is generally not counted as unemployed in the standard definition, and unemployment is not the same as total labour force size.', tag: 'Unemployment' },
    { q: 'Cyclical unemployment is caused by:', options: ['A downturn or recession in the economic cycle reducing demand for labour', 'Workers lacking the skills needed for available jobs', 'Workers voluntarily choosing not to work', 'Seasonal changes in demand, such as in tourism'], correct: 0, explain: 'Cyclical unemployment rises during an economic downturn or recession, when overall demand — and therefore demand for labour — falls.', misconception: 'A skills mismatch describes structural unemployment, and seasonal changes describe seasonal unemployment, both distinct causes from cyclical unemployment.', tag: 'Unemployment' },
    { q: 'Structural unemployment occurs when:', options: ['Workers’ skills no longer match the jobs available, often due to changes in industry', 'The whole economy enters a recession', 'Workers are between jobs for a short period', 'Demand for labour changes with the seasons'], correct: 0, explain: 'Structural unemployment arises from a mismatch between workers’ skills and the jobs available, often due to long-term shifts such as industrial decline or automation.', misconception: 'A short-term recession causes cyclical, not structural, unemployment — structural unemployment reflects a deeper skills or industry mismatch.', tag: 'Unemployment' },
    { q: 'Frictional unemployment refers to:', options: ['The short-term unemployment of workers moving between jobs', 'Unemployment caused entirely by a recession', 'Workers who never intend to look for a job', 'Unemployment caused only by the seasons'], correct: 0, explain: 'Frictional unemployment is short-term and occurs naturally as workers move between jobs, searching for a better match.', misconception: 'Frictional unemployment is a normal, short-term feature of any labour market, not something caused specifically by a recession or the seasons.', tag: 'Unemployment' },
    { q: 'A likely impact of high unemployment on government finances is:', options: ['Higher spending on benefits and lower tax revenue', 'Lower spending on benefits and higher tax revenue', 'No effect on government finances at all', 'Guaranteed budget surpluses'], correct: 0, explain: 'Higher unemployment typically increases government spending on unemployment benefits while reducing income tax revenue, worsening the government’s fiscal position.', misconception: 'The effect works in the opposite direction to what might be assumed — unemployment increases benefit spending and reduces, not increases, tax revenue.', tag: 'Unemployment' },
    { q: 'The current account of the balance of payments records:', options: ['A country’s trade in goods and services, plus income and transfers, with the rest of the world', 'Only a country’s exports of goods', 'A country’s total government spending', 'A country’s exchange rate against every other currency'], correct: 0, explain: 'The current account records trade in goods (visibles) and services (invisibles), along with income flows and transfers between a country and the rest of the world.', misconception: 'The current account covers a wider range of international transactions than exports alone, and it is distinct from government spending or exchange rate levels.', tag: 'Balance of payments' },
    { q: 'A current account deficit occurs when:', options: ['The value of a country’s imports (plus other outflows) exceeds the value of its exports (plus other inflows)', 'A country exports more than it imports', 'A country has zero international trade', 'The government runs a budget surplus'], correct: 0, explain: 'A current account deficit means more money is flowing out of a country (through imports and other payments) than is flowing in (through exports and receipts).', misconception: 'Exports exceeding imports would create a current account surplus, not a deficit — the deficit describes the opposite trade balance.', tag: 'Balance of payments' },
    { q: '"Visibles" in the balance of payments refer to trade in:', options: ['Physical, tangible goods, such as cars or clothing', 'Services, such as banking or tourism', 'Currency exchange only', 'Government transfer payments'], correct: 0, explain: 'Visibles refer to trade in physical goods that can be seen and counted crossing borders, such as manufactured products or raw materials.', misconception: 'Trade in services, such as banking, insurance or tourism, is classified as invisibles, not visibles.', tag: 'Balance of payments' },
    { q: 'A fall in the price of domestically produced goods relative to foreign goods would tend to:', options: ['Improve the current account, as exports become more competitive and imports less attractive', 'Worsen the current account by making exports more expensive', 'Have no effect on trade flows', 'Only affect the exchange rate, not trade'], correct: 0, explain: 'If domestic goods become relatively cheaper, exports are likely to rise (more competitive abroad) and imports may fall (domestic goods more attractive at home), improving the current account balance.', misconception: 'Cheaper domestic goods make exports more, not less, competitive, which tends to improve rather than worsen the current account.', tag: 'Balance of payments' },
    { q: 'A common way businesses cause damage to the environment is through:', options: ['Air, water and noise pollution from production processes', 'Reducing the price of their products', 'Paying higher wages to staff', 'Increasing the number of jobs created'], correct: 0, explain: 'Business production can generate various forms of pollution — air, water, noise and visual — that damage the environment.', misconception: 'Pricing, wages and job creation are business decisions unrelated directly to environmental damage, which usually stems from production processes and pollution.', tag: 'Protecting the environment' },
    { q: 'A government could use a tax to help protect the environment by:', options: ['Taxing polluting activities to raise their cost and discourage them', 'Removing all taxes on polluting businesses', 'Subsidising the most polluting industries', 'Banning all forms of taxation'], correct: 0, explain: 'Taxing a polluting activity raises its cost, giving firms and consumers a financial incentive to reduce that activity and its environmental damage.', misconception: 'Removing taxes or subsidising pollution would likely increase, not reduce, environmental damage — a tax is used to discourage harmful activity.', tag: 'Protecting the environment' },
    { q: 'Regulation, such as legal limits on emissions, can help protect the environment by:', options: ['Legally restricting the amount of pollution a business is allowed to produce', 'Guaranteeing zero cost to businesses', 'Removing the need for any environmental monitoring', 'Increasing pollution levels to boost output'], correct: 0, explain: 'Environmental regulation sets legal limits (for example on emissions) that businesses must comply with, directly restricting environmental damage.', misconception: 'Regulation restricts, rather than removes the need to monitor, environmental harm, and it does not eliminate all costs to businesses — compliance often has a cost.', tag: 'Protecting the environment' },
    { q: 'Income inequality refers to:', options: ['Differences in the level of income between individuals or households in an economy', 'A situation where everyone earns exactly the same income', 'The total income earned by a country in a year', 'A measure of a country’s exchange rate'], correct: 0, explain: 'Income inequality describes the extent to which income is unevenly distributed across individuals or households in an economy.', misconception: 'Income inequality is about differences between people’s incomes, not the total value of national income (which relates to GDP) or exchange rates.', tag: 'Redistribution of income' },
    { q: 'Absolute poverty refers to:', options: ['Not having enough income to afford the basic necessities of life, such as food, shelter and clean water', 'Having a lower income than the average person in a country', 'Any income below the national average wage', 'A temporary lack of luxury goods'], correct: 0, explain: 'Absolute poverty means lacking the minimum income needed to meet basic survival needs, regardless of what others in society have.', misconception: 'Having a lower income than others (a comparative measure) describes relative poverty, not absolute poverty, which is about failing to meet basic needs.', tag: 'Redistribution of income' },
    { q: 'Relative poverty refers to:', options: ['Having a significantly lower income than the average or typical income in a society', 'Not having enough income to survive at all', 'Earning exactly the national average income', 'A fixed, universal poverty line used in every country'], correct: 0, explain: 'Relative poverty compares an individual’s or household’s income to the average income in their own society, rather than to an absolute survival threshold.', misconception: 'Relative poverty is about comparison to others in the same society, unlike absolute poverty, which is about meeting a fixed basic-needs threshold.', tag: 'Redistribution of income' },
    { q: 'Progressive taxation can help reduce income inequality because:', options: ['Those on higher incomes pay a higher percentage of their income in tax', 'Everyone pays exactly the same amount of tax regardless of income', 'Only the poorest pay any tax at all', 'It removes all government revenue from taxation'], correct: 0, explain: 'Under a progressive tax system, the percentage of income paid in tax rises as income rises, helping to redistribute income from higher to lower earners.', misconception: 'A system where everyone pays the same amount (or rate) is not progressive — progressive taxation specifically requires the tax burden to rise with income.', tag: 'Redistribution of income' },
    { q: 'Fiscal policy refers to government decisions about:', options: ['Taxation and government spending', 'Setting interest rates and the money supply', 'Regulating individual businesses only', 'Setting the exchange rate directly'], correct: 0, explain: 'Fiscal policy involves government decisions on how much to tax and how much to spend.', misconception: 'Setting interest rates and controlling the money supply is monetary policy, a separate tool typically controlled by a central bank.', tag: 'Fiscal policy' },
    { q: 'A fiscal deficit occurs when:', options: ['Government spending exceeds government revenue in a given period', 'Government revenue exceeds government spending', 'A government collects no tax at all', 'A country has a current account surplus'], correct: 0, explain: 'A fiscal (budget) deficit occurs when government spending is greater than the tax and other revenue it collects over a period, usually a year.', misconception: 'Revenue exceeding spending would describe a fiscal surplus, the opposite situation to a deficit.', tag: 'Fiscal policy' },
    { q: 'Direct taxes are taxes levied directly on:', options: ['Income or wealth, such as income tax', 'Spending on goods and services, such as VAT', 'Imported goods only', 'Company logos'], correct: 0, explain: 'Direct taxes, such as income tax or corporation tax, are charged directly on income, profit or wealth.', misconception: 'Taxes on spending, such as VAT, are indirect taxes, not direct taxes, since they are collected via the price of goods and services.', tag: 'Fiscal policy' },
    { q: 'Expansionary fiscal policy, such as increased government spending, is typically used to:', options: ['Boost economic growth and reduce unemployment during a downturn', 'Reduce inflation during a boom', 'Guarantee a fiscal surplus every year', 'Eliminate all government debt immediately'], correct: 0, explain: 'Expansionary fiscal policy (higher spending and/or lower taxes) aims to stimulate demand, output and employment, often used during an economic downturn.', misconception: 'Reducing inflation during a boom would typically call for contractionary, not expansionary, fiscal policy.', tag: 'Fiscal policy' },
    { q: 'Monetary policy refers to decisions about:', options: ['Interest rates and the money supply, typically set by a central bank', 'Government taxation and spending', 'Business regulation and legislation', 'Trade agreements between countries'], correct: 0, explain: 'Monetary policy involves controlling interest rates and the money supply, usually managed by a country’s central bank.', misconception: 'Taxation and spending decisions form fiscal policy, a separate government tool from monetary policy.', tag: 'Monetary policy' },
    { q: 'A rise in interest rates set by a central bank would typically:', options: ['Increase the cost of borrowing, reducing consumer spending and business investment', 'Reduce the cost of borrowing for everyone', 'Have no effect on consumer spending', 'Guarantee an increase in economic growth'], correct: 0, explain: 'Higher interest rates raise the cost of borrowing (and increase the reward for saving), which tends to reduce consumer spending and business investment, cooling the economy.', misconception: 'A rise in interest rates increases, rather than decreases, borrowing costs, and this tends to slow, not boost, economic growth.', tag: 'Monetary policy' },
    { q: 'Central banks often raise interest rates in order to:', options: ['Reduce inflationary pressure by cooling consumer and business spending', 'Increase inflation as quickly as possible', 'Guarantee falling unemployment', 'Eliminate the need for fiscal policy'], correct: 0, explain: 'Raising interest rates is a common monetary policy tool used to reduce demand in the economy and control inflation.', misconception: 'Higher interest rates are used to reduce, not increase, inflationary pressure by discouraging spending and borrowing.', tag: 'Monetary policy' },
    { q: 'Supply-side policy aims to:', options: ['Increase the productive capacity and efficiency of an economy', 'Directly control the exchange rate', 'Only affect short-term consumer demand', 'Reduce a country’s population'], correct: 0, explain: 'Supply-side policies aim to improve the long-term productive potential of an economy, for example through education, infrastructure or deregulation.', misconception: 'Supply-side policy targets the productive (supply) side of the economy over the long term, unlike demand-side (fiscal/monetary) policies aimed at short-term demand.', tag: 'Supply-side policy' },
    { q: 'Investment in education and training is an example of a supply-side policy because it can:', options: ['Improve the skills and productivity of the workforce over the long term', 'Directly increase government spending on benefits', 'Immediately reduce interest rates', 'Only affect the exchange rate'], correct: 0, explain: 'Education and training raise workers’ skills and productivity, improving the economy’s long-run productive capacity — a supply-side effect.', misconception: 'Education/training policy works through improving productive capacity, not through directly adjusting interest rates, benefits, or the exchange rate.', tag: 'Supply-side policy' },
    { q: 'Deregulation, as a supply-side policy, involves:', options: ['Reducing government rules and restrictions on businesses to encourage efficiency and competition', 'Increasing the amount of regulation on all businesses', 'Nationalising all private businesses', 'Fixing all prices in the economy'], correct: 0, explain: 'Deregulation reduces red tape and restrictions on businesses, aiming to increase competition, efficiency and enterprise.', misconception: 'Deregulation reduces, rather than increases, the regulatory burden on businesses — more regulation would be the opposite policy.', tag: 'Supply-side policy' },
    { q: 'Lower rates of income tax, used as a supply-side policy, aim to:', options: ['Provide a greater incentive for people to work and increase their effort', 'Immediately increase inflation', 'Guarantee a fiscal surplus', 'Reduce the size of the labour force'], correct: 0, explain: 'Lower income tax rates can increase the financial reward from working, potentially encouraging more people to work or to work more hours, supporting productive capacity.', misconception: 'Lower income tax is intended to incentivise, not discourage, work — it targets labour supply, not directly inflation or the budget balance.', tag: 'Supply-side policy' },
    { q: 'A key advantage of using government regulation to control business behaviour is that it:', options: ['Can set clear, enforceable legal limits that businesses must follow', 'Always has zero cost to implement or enforce', 'Guarantees perfect compliance from every business', 'Removes the need for any government involvement at all'], correct: 0, explain: 'Regulation provides clear, legally binding rules that businesses must follow, which can be an effective way to control harmful behaviour.', misconception: 'Regulation still involves enforcement costs and is not guaranteed to achieve perfect compliance — these are real limitations of the policy.', tag: 'Government controls' },
    { q: 'A key disadvantage of fines as a form of government control is that:', options: ['Large firms may treat a fine as simply a cost of doing business if it is too low relative to their profits', 'Fines always eliminate the harmful behaviour completely', 'Fines are illegal in most countries', 'Fines have no effect on business behaviour whatsoever'], correct: 0, explain: 'If a fine is too small relative to a firm’s profits, the firm may simply accept it as a cost of doing business rather than change its behaviour.', misconception: 'Fines can be an effective deterrent, but they don’t guarantee behaviour change — their effectiveness depends on being set high enough relative to the firm’s profits.', tag: 'Government controls' },
    { q: 'There is often a trade-off between reducing unemployment and controlling inflation because:', options: ['Policies that boost demand to cut unemployment can also increase inflationary pressure', 'Unemployment and inflation are always completely unrelated', 'Reducing unemployment always reduces inflation at the same time', 'Government can never influence either variable'], correct: 0, explain: 'Expansionary policies that reduce unemployment by boosting demand can also increase inflationary pressure, illustrating a classic policy trade-off.', misconception: 'These two objectives are often linked through a trade-off, not entirely separate or automatically moving in the same direction.', tag: 'Objectives & policy trade-offs' },
    { q: 'A trade-off between economic growth and the environment can occur because:', options: ['Higher economic growth, driven by increased production, can increase pollution and resource use', 'Economic growth always improves the environment automatically', 'The environment has no link to economic activity', 'Environmental protection guarantees faster economic growth'], correct: 0, explain: 'Growth driven by increased production and consumption can increase pollution and depletion of resources, creating a potential conflict between growth and environmental goals.', misconception: 'Growth does not automatically benefit the environment — expanding output can increase environmental harm, creating a genuine policy trade-off.', tag: 'Objectives & policy trade-offs' },
    { q: 'A policy that successfully boosts economic growth may also risk:', options: ['Higher inflation, if demand rises faster than the economy’s productive capacity', 'Guaranteed zero unemployment forever', 'An automatic fall in all prices', 'The complete elimination of the economic cycle'], correct: 0, explain: 'If growth is driven by demand rising faster than supply can expand, this can create inflationary pressure — a common growth-inflation trade-off.', misconception: 'Growth policies can create inflation risk rather than guaranteeing permanently low unemployment or the end of the economic cycle.', tag: 'Objectives & policy trade-offs' }
  ];

